class Private:
    """The GLib.Private struct is an opaque data structure to represent a
    thread-local data key. It is approximately equivalent to the
    pthread_setspecific()/pthread_getspecific() APIs on POSIX and to
    TlsSetValue()/TlsGetValue() on Windows.

    If you don't already know why you might want this functionality,
    then you probably don't need it.

    GLib.Private is a very limited resource (as far as 128 per program,
    shared between all libraries). It is also not possible to destroy a
    GLib.Private after it has been used. As such, it is only ever acceptable
    to use GLib.Private in static scope, and even then sparingly so.

    See G_PRIVATE_INIT() for a couple of examples.

    The GLib.Private structure should be considered opaque.  It should only
    be accessed via the g_private_ functions.
    """
    def get(self) -> _Any:
        """Returns the current value of the thread local variable self.

        If the value has not yet been set in this thread, None is returned.
        Values are never copied between threads (when a new thread is
        created, for example).

        Returns:
            the thread-local value
        """
    def replace(self, value: _Any = ...) -> None:
        """Sets the thread local variable self to have the value value in the
        current thread.

        This function differs from GLib.Private.set() in the following way: if
        the previous value was non-None then the GLib.DestroyNotify handler for
        self is run on it.

        Args:
            value: the new value
        """
    def set(self, value: _Any = ...) -> None:
        """Sets the thread local variable self to have the value value in the
        current thread.

        This function differs from GLib.Private.replace() in the following way:
        the GLib.DestroyNotify for self is not called on the old value.

        Args:
            value: the new value
        """

